WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.595
Two pods can each start the nightly settlement batch, which builds the payout file for the bank.

2
00:00:08.595 --> 00:00:11.481
If both run it, every merchant gets paid twice.

3
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.881
So they take a lock in Redis.

4
00:00:13.881 --> 00:00:17.943
Pod A sends a SET command with two options, NX and PX.

5
00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:21.851
NX means: only set the key if it doesn't exist yet.

6
00:00:21.851 --> 00:00:24.452
The key is free, so A gets the lock.

7
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:25.813
PX sets a lease.

8
00:00:25.813 --> 00:00:32.979
Redis deletes the key by itself after 30 seconds, so a pod that crashes can't hold the lock forever.

9
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.189
Pod B sends the same command.

10
00:00:35.189 --> 00:00:38.824
The key already exists, so B gets nothing, and waits.

11
00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:40.133
Now A freezes.

12
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:45.397
A 35-second garbage collection pause halts every thread in its JVM.

13
00:00:45.397 --> 00:00:48.461
Pod A has no idea that time is passing.

14
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:49.902
30 seconds go by.

15
00:00:49.902 --> 00:00:53.245
The lease runs out, and Redis deletes the key.

16
00:00:53.245 --> 00:00:56.516
As far as Redis knows, nobody holds the lock.

17
00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.308
B asks again and gets the lock.

18
00:00:59.308 --> 00:01:01.143
B writes the payout file.

19
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:02.404
Then A wakes up.

20
00:01:02.404 --> 00:01:04.869
From where A stands, nothing happened.

21
00:01:04.869 --> 00:01:09.280
It still thinks it holds the lock, so it writes the payout file too.

22
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.077
A lease can't prevent this.

23
00:01:11.077 --> 00:01:15.807
Checking the lock first doesn't help: the pause can strike right after the check.

24
00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:17.783
The fix is a fencing token.

25
00:01:17.783 --> 00:01:24.132
The script that takes the lock also adds one to a counter in Redis, and hands back the new number.

26
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:25.338
Run it again.

27
00:01:25.338 --> 00:01:28.970
Pod A gets the lock with token 33, then pauses.

28
00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:33.143
The lease runs out, and B gets the lock with token 34.

29
00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:39.920
Every write carries its token, and the store remembers the highest token it has accepted.

30
00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.172
B writes with 34.

31
00:01:41.172 --> 00:01:41.835
Accepted.

32
00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:49.433
When A wakes up and writes with 33, the store sees that 33 is lower than 34, and refuses the write.

33
00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:51.426
The lock alone is not enough.

34
00:01:51.426 --> 00:01:55.876
The store has to check the token and refuse any write with an older one.
